    "## Stretch Challenge (Hard) — Canteen Rush Hour System\n",
    "\n",
    "You've already handled Python before, so this one skips the warm-up: it's B1–B10 and Part C combined into a single messier, real-world-shaped problem. Nothing here uses any concept you haven't already touched today — the difficulty is entirely in **combining them correctly** and **handling the edge cases without letting one bad order crash the whole run**.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Scenario\n",
    "It's lunch rush. Multiple customers place orders in quick succession. Some orders will have mistakes (an item not on the menu, or not enough cash). Your program must process the whole rush, skip and log the bad orders, and still produce an accurate summary at the end.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Setup\n",
    "```python\n",
    "menu = {\n",
    "    \"Samosa\": {\"price\": 15.0, \"category\": \"Snacks\"},\n",
    "    \"Vada\": {\"price\": 12.0, \"category\": \"Snacks\"},\n",
    "    \"Tea\": {\"price\": 10.0, \"category\": \"Beverages\"},\n",
    "    \"Cold Coffee\": {\"price\": 30.0, \"category\": \"Beverages\"},\n",
    "    \"Dosa\": {\"price\": 40.0, \"category\": \"Meals\"},\n",
    "    \"Idli\": {\"price\": 20.0, \"category\": \"Meals\"}\n",
    "}\n",
    "\n",
    "orders = [\n",
    "    {\"customer\": \"Ravi\",   \"cart\": [\"Samosa\", \"Tea\"],       \"tier\": \"regular\", \"hour\": 13, \"is_parcel\": False, \"cash_paid\": 30},\n",
    "    {\"customer\": \"Ananya\", \"cart\": [\"Dosa\", \"Cold Coffee\"], \"tier\": \"gold\",    \"hour\": 12, \"is_parcel\": True,  \"cash_paid\": 100},\n",
    "    {\"customer\": \"Kiran\",  \"cart\": [\"Vada\", \"Idli\"],        \"tier\": \"silver\",  \"hour\": 14, \"is_parcel\": False, \"cash_paid\": 45},\n",
    "    {\"customer\": \"Meena\",  \"cart\": [\"Pizza\"],               \"tier\": \"regular\", \"hour\": 13, \"is_parcel\": False, \"cash_paid\": 100},\n",
    "    {\"customer\": \"Arjun\",  \"cart\": [\"Dosa\", \"Dosa\", \"Tea\"], \"tier\": \"gold\",    \"hour\": 12, \"is_parcel\": False, \"cash_paid\": 95},\n",
    "    {\"customer\": \"Divya\",  \"cart\": [\"Tea\"],                 \"tier\": \"regular\", \"hour\": 15, \"is_parcel\": False, \"cash_paid\": 5},\n",
    "]\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### Requirements\n",
    "\n",
    "Write `process_order(order, menu)` and a driver loop that, across all orders in `orders`:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Peak-hour surcharge** — if `hour` is 12 or 13 (lunch rush), add an 8% surcharge on the subtotal *before* GST. Outside those hours, no surcharge.\n",
    "2. **Two competing discounts, pick the better one** — a membership discount (`regular`: 0%, `silver`: 5%, `gold`: 12%) *and* the loyalty discount from earlier (subtotal > 200 → 10%, > 100 → 5%, else 0%). Apply whichever discount is **larger**, never both stacked.\n",
    "3. **GST (5%)** applies after the discount, and a **Rs.5 packing charge** if `is_parcel` is True — same as before.\n",
    "4. **Bad orders must not stop the run.** If a cart item isn't in `menu`, catch it (`KeyError`), log the order to `failed_transactions.txt` with the reason, and move on to the next order. Same for insufficient cash (`ValueError`) — don't let the loop die.\n",
    "5. **Successful orders** get appended to `rush_hour_log.txt` as `\"<customer>: Rs.<total>\"`.\n",
    "6. **End-of-rush summary**, printed (not just written to file):\n",
    "   - Total revenue collected across all successful orders.\n",
    "   - Revenue broken down **by category** (Snacks / Beverages / Meals) — build this without importing anything, using a plain dictionary you update as you go.\n",
    "   - The single **best-selling item** by total quantity sold across all orders (again, no imports — track counts in a dictionary).\n",
    "   - How many orders **failed**, and why.\n",
    "\n",
    "Try to get through all 5 sample orders (including the two broken ones) without your program stopping partway.\n"
   ]
  },
